Design and prior art
--------------------
routwatch is the fourth tool in a family that already contained netwatch,
syswatch and diskwatch. All three are by Matt Hartley (matthart1983) and are
MIT-licensed:

  netwatch   https://github.com/matthart1983/netwatch
  syswatch   https://github.com/matthart1983/syswatch
  diskwatch  https://github.com/matthart1983/diskwatch

From them come the conventions this tool deliberately follows, and which its
users expect because they run the other three side by side: the
~/.config/<tool>/config.toml shape with the same keys, `theme = "terminal"`
defaulting to the terminal's own palette, the V/L/F view cycle, `--tab` and
`--graph` spellings, the `doctor --json` capability schema (id/state/reason/
detail/next_check), shell completion emitted by the binary itself, and the
habit of stating a limitation in the UI instead of showing a plausible zero.

No source code from syswatch or diskwatch was copied into this repository.

The feature set and the router behaviour it encodes (endpoints, band masks,
what the H122-373 firmware does and does not answer) come from HManagerPy
2.1.0, the Electron application this tool replaces, written by RoOt-SeC.


Theme palettes — derived code
-----------------------------
src/theme.rs carries eight palettes whose colour values are taken byte-for-byte
from netwatch's src/theme.rs (the slot names are routwatch's own, the hex values
are not ours). netwatch is Copyright (c) Matt Hartley and distributed under the
MIT license reproduced below. Keeping the values identical is deliberate: the
same theme must look the same in all four tools.

